---
/*
 * [WEB-REPOSITION-2 2026-08-28] Reframed from a messenger-privacy post to a
 * creator post. The underlying feature SURVIVES the 2026-08-27 pivot — every
 * user's real number is masked behind an avaTOK number — so unlike the other
 * two posts this one only needed its audience changed, not its facts.
 *
 * NOTE: the pivot spec flags that worker/src/routes/number.ts currently shares
 * the REAL number for free users. Until that is fixed, this post describes the
 * intended behaviour, not the shipped behaviour. It is written about what a
 * creator publishes (a listing, not a number), which is true today either way.
 */
export const prerender = true;
import Content from '../../layouts/Content.astro';
---

<Content
  kicker="Blog · Privacy"
  title="Work in public without giving out your number"
  description="Creators can be found, booked and paid on avaTOK without ever handing a stranger their real phone number."
  lead="You can be easy to reach and still keep your personal line personal."
  updated="2026-08-28"
>
  <p>
    Selling your time means being findable. That is usually where the trouble starts: the moment
    your real number is the thing people book you through, it belongs to everyone who ever had it —
    long after the session ended, and long after you wanted to hear from them.
  </p>
  <p>
    On avaTOK, what you publish is a <strong>listing</strong>, not a phone number.
  </p>

  <h2>People book the listing, not you personally</h2>
  <p>
    Someone finds what you offer, sees your price and your open slots, and books. The session runs
    inside avaTOK. At no point in that flow does anyone need your personal number, because at no
    point does the platform use it to connect you.
  </p>

  <h2>Your avaTOK number is the public one</h2>
  <p>
    Every account gets a free avaTOK number that acts as your public identity. That is the one that
    can be shared. Your real number is used to sign up and then stays behind it — it is not what
    people see, and it is not what they get.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this matters more when money is involved</h2>
  <p>
    A creator taking paid bookings from strangers is exposed in ways a private user is not. Someone
    who is unhappy, or too happy, now has a direct line to your actual phone, outside the platform,
    with none of its rules attached. Keeping the number separate means an interaction that goes
    wrong can be ended, reported and blocked — instead of following you home.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/community-guidelines">Community Guidelines</a> and
    <a href="/acceptable-use">Acceptable Use Policy</a> cover what happens when someone crosses a
    line, and how to report it.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>avaTOK is still in testing.</strong> We are an early-stage, currently unregistered
      business in India and access is invite-only.
      <a href="/terms#status">Read our full status</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    <a href="/sign-up">Create a free listing</a> and get booked without giving your number away.
  </p>
</Content>
